A thread that loses its share in a call gives the processor up at once, not at the end of its turn.
A revoke that unbinds its caller and stops for an interrupt would switch threads in the middle of the call,
which the host, making the call again at once, does not;
a call stopped for an interrupt is made again before anything else runs.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-grain32 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: a call was preempted before it took anything away

diff --git a/kernel/syscall.c b/kernel/syscall.c
--- a/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -771,7 +771,7 @@ void syscall_dispatch(struct thread *t)
      * A thread that waits gives the processor up.
      * One that lost its share finishes its turn, as a preempted call is made again before the tick switches.
      */
-    if (current->state != THREAD_READY) {
+    if (current->state != THREAD_READY || current->share.type != CAP_BOUND) {
         sched_run_next();
     }
     if (debug_trace) {
